{"title":"2007 iTest Problems/Problem 43","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_43&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Bored of working on her computational linguistics thesis, Erin enters some three-digit integers into a spreadsheet, then manipulates the cells a bit until her spreadsheet calculates each of the following one hundred <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-digit integers: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/3/393b21d8c1383b5915156169236df5d1ba86a2c8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}700\\cdot 712\\,\\cdot\\, &amp;718+320,\\\\701\\cdot 713\\,\\cdot\\, &amp;719+320,\\\\ 702\\cdot 714\\,\\cdot\\, &amp;720+320,\\\\&amp;\\vdots\\\\798\\cdot 810\\,\\cdot\\, &amp;816+320,\\\\799\\cdot 811\\,\\cdot\\, &amp;817+320.\\end{align*}\" width=\"158\" height=\"154\" />\n</p><p>She notes that two of them have exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> positive divisors each. Find the common prime divisor of those two integers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_43&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that each number can be written in form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/8/f38b5b11b59c0c0c723caf34ff9ba0a2e2beb279.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n(n+12)(n+18) + 320$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"190\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is an integer from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/4/b640caf462417d59208b590f634dd8de89b8a96f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$700$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/8/7981979dffcf3cac27cdf1204e72d58611bb36ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$799$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />.  Expanding the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomial</a> results in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/3/1c3f385f3a4de4c704e8cd533853fcf834fc8b34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^3 + 30n^2 + 216n + 320$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"186\" height=\"16\" />, and factoring that results in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/c/e4cf0f15d3e7e8e7c080cf3839a8d8514fe0d05f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n+2)(n+8)(n+20)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"176\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nIf a number has eight positive divisors, then it’s <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_factorization\" title=\"Prime factorization\">prime factorization</a> is in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/2/9c21ef0e9ff48642604a2929970618d8728a323b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^7$\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/d/88dd4d37746a9e0c42a22d76366c4ae15b7d6912.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^3b$\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96a81938ca5e71faa6894d9d52ac776c9efbe75d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$abc$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> are primes.  The most likely option is three different <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Prime\">prime numbers</a> being multiplied together.\n</p><p><br />\nAfter finding the prime numbers from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/2/ae2f549965059c7e2b65c4366cfe542fc8fb8789.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$702$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/0/460b4e51e56ed86ea656ff5e50d721283b2dc320.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$819$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />, the values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> that work are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/5/5158b33f75bd8a343cc87f7fbf303e1caa80878d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$731$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/9/4c9b8fec7bcf936dce6d1d8a43d8d4f2c651fb6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$749$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> (after finding one value, note that both share a factor to find the other value).  Thus, the prime factorizations of the two numbers are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a74109bc66f4a0b5a3b239bdfefddc3b29434117.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$733 \\cdot 739 \\cdot 751$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"110\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/a/cda2d60a2619f54f6a61faff31b80125191bae3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$751 \\cdot 757 \\cdot 769$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"107\" height=\"13\" />, so the common prime divisor of the two is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/3/963260483ac8b5128ac947901c026f3d65fbf041.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{751}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_43&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest\" title=\"2007 iTest\">2007 iTest</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems\" title=\"2007 iTest Problems\">(Problems</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Answer_Key\" title=\"2007 iTest Answer Key\">Answer Key)</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_42\" title=\"2007 iTest Problems/Problem 42\">Problem 42</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_44\" title=\"2007 iTest Problems/Problem 44\">Problem 44</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2007 iTest Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2007_iTest_Problems